{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Let me organize the scope. Things to do:\n1. Write `linkcheck.py` — a standard-library-only checker for local Markdown links inside the `docs/` folder.\n2. Broken links reported one per line as `file:line: target`.\n3. Exit code 1 if there are broken links.\n4. Create `docs/` with three small sample files, one of which has a broken link.\n5. Run it once.\n\nLet me check the working directory first.\n\nPlan:\n- `linkcheck.py`: scan `docs/**/*.md`, parse Markdown links (both `[text](target)` inline and `[text]: target` reference links), skip external URLs (http://, https://, mailto:), handle anchors `#...` (skip or check in-file?), local relative links resolve relative to the file's directory. Report `file:line: target` for broken ones. Exit 1 if any are broken.\n\nKeep it short. Use `re` for parsing. Handle inline links `[text](target)` and image links `![alt](src)`. Skip external schemes. Strip anchor fragments for file existence checks (or handle same-file anchors).\n\nSimple approach:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check local Markdown links in docs/.\"\"\"\nimport re\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nDOCS = Path(\"docs\")\nLINK_RE = re.compile(r'(?<!\\!)\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(([^)\\s]+(?:\\s+\"[^\"]*\")?)\\)|!\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(([^)\\s]+)\\)')\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: `re.compile(r'\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)')` — captures both links and images (image links should also be checked, but images are often binary; keep it simple and check them all, or just links? \"local Markdown links\" — check link targets; images could be included too. Keep it simple: just `\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(...\\)` matches both, check all).\n\nAlso strip angle brackets and titles: `target.split()[0]` handles `(target \"title\")`. Handle `<...>`: if target starts with `<`, strip it.\n\nSkip external: `re.match(r'[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9+.-]*:', target)` — any scheme (http, https, mailto). Also skip pure anchors `#...`.\n\nResolve: `path = (docs_dir / md_file.parent / target).resolve()` — no wait, target is relative to the file's directory. `base = md_file.parent / target`. Strip the fragment before checking existence. For relative resolution, if target contains `..` we may need resolve; but if the docs directory doesn't exist, `.resolve()` fails — check existence with `(base).exists()` — but URL-encoded characters... keep it simple, don't over-engineer. Use `Path(base)` and check `exists()`. Actually, to be safe against absolute paths starting with `/`, `Path(base)` handles it fine; if the target is an absolute path, skip it (out of scope). Keep it short.\n\nLine numbers: iterate lines with enumerate starting at 1. Report relative path from docs? \"file:line: target\" — use the file path as found (e.g., `docs/a.md:3: b.md`). Use a path relative to the current directory or relative to docs. Since we scan docs/, print as `docs/...` (relative to CWD).\n\nFenced code blocks: should skip links inside code fences — that's good practice and easy: track ```` fences. Keep it short, but do it.\n\nReference-style links `[text]: target` — include? \"local Markdown links\" — inline links are the core. Including reference definitions is cheap: lines matching `^\\s*\\[[^\\]]+\\]:\\s*(\\S+)`. Include it.\n\nAlso handle `**bold**` etc. — not needed.\n\nExit code: 1 if any broken, 0 otherwise.\n\nSample files:\n- `docs/index.md` — links to `guide.md` (OK) and `broken.md` (broken → broken link).\n- `docs/guide.md` — links to `index.md`, maybe an external link too.\n- `docs/about.md` — or maybe a third file that index links to; the broken link should be exactly one. \"one link broken\" — one broken link. So index.md has: `[Guide](guide.md)` OK, `[Missing](missing.md)` broken. guide.md links to index.md (OK). Third file about.md, with a link... but then index should link to about.md too, otherwise about.md is orphaned. That's fine — the checker scans all files, and about.md can contain a link to index.md (OK). Broken link exactly one.\n\nRun: `python3 linkcheck.py` → prints `docs/index.md:2: missing.md` (or similar), exit code 1.\n\nThe repo is \"Rust and TypeScript\" — but the task is to write a Python script, so that's fine; scope is explicitly decided.\n\nLet me check the working directory first.\n","seq":416,"t":53030,"text":"","timings":{"cache_n":0,"draft_n":2905,"draft_n_accepted":715,"predicted_ms":14330.0,"predicted_n":1130,"prompt_ms":1900.0,"prompt_n":2312},"to_request":3,"usage":{"cached_tokens":0,"completion_tokens":1130,"prompt_tokens":2312}}
