{"finish_reason":"tool_calls","kind":"response","reasoning":"Note: the fence toggle in `main` has a subtle issue: the fence state is maintained per file — no problem, since we reset `in_fence = False` for each file. Good.\n\nAnother subtle point: `md.parent / t` — if `t` is an absolute path (starts with `/`), pathlib's `/` returns the absolute path. This is actually the correct behavior. OK.\n\nNext, sample files. Three small files, with exactly one broken link:\n\ndocs/index.md:\n```\n# Docs\n\n- [Guide](guide.md)\n- [About](about.md)\n- [Missing](missing.md)\n```\n\ndocs/guide.md:\n```\n# Guide\n\nBack to [index](index.md). See [MDN](https://developer.mozilla.org) for reference syntax.\n\n```md\n[not a real link](nope.md)\n```\n```\n\nWait — the fenced block in guide.md contains a link to nope.md; since the checker skips fenced blocks, it won't be reported. This demonstrates that feature, but the requirement says \"one broken link\" — meaning the only broken link that gets reported is missing.md. The fenced one gets skipped so it's fine, and it also serves as a test of the fence handling. Good.\n\ndocs/about.md:\n```\n# About\n\n[Back to index](index.md#top) — fragment links are checked...\n```\n\nHmm, `index.md#top` — the file part `index.md` exists, so it's OK. I won't verify anchor existence (I didn't include that; keep it simple). Good.\n\nAfter that, run `python3 linkcheck.py; echo \"exit: $?\"`.\n","seq":623,"t":69490,"text":"\n\nNow the sample docs — three files, exactly one broken link (plus a fenced code block that must be ignored):\n\n\n\n","timings":{"cache_n":4096,"draft_n":1183,"draft_n_accepted":419,"predicted_ms":6020.0,"predicted_n":588,"prompt_ms":740.0,"prompt_n":664},"to_request":481,"usage":{"cached_tokens":4096,"completion_tokens":588,"prompt_tokens":4760}}
